  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to use a structured initialization with periodic-like patterns and a hybrid optimization strategy combining gradient-based methods with perturbation to escape local minima. This approach addresses the non-convexity of the problem by leveraging smooth approximations of the max operator and improving initial configurations to avoid poor local optima. The bottleneck of the previous method\u2014getting stuck in local minima\u2014will be mitigated by introducing smooth surrogates and diverse perturbations.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe function h must satisfy:\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n- \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nThe objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx, where the integral is computed numerically using np.correlate. To handle the non-differentiable max operation, we approximate it with a smooth surrogate:\n\nC\u2085_approx = (1/\u03b1) * ln(\u03a3 exp(\u03b1 * overlap_k)) \n\nwhere \u03b1 is a large constant (e.g., 1000) to approximate the max. This allows gradient-based optimization. We use the SLSQP method with bounds and constraints to ensure feasibility. The surrogates are evaluated alongside the exact evaluator for final checks.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n- Start with a structured initialization: generate h from a sinusoidal pattern scaled to meet the integral constraint. Use h(x) = 0.5 * (1 + cos(\u03c0 x)) normalized to \u222b h(x) dx = 1.\n- Use COBYLA for optimization: minimize the smooth surrogate C\u2085_approx.\n- During optimization, apply a gradient descent step followed by a projection to ensure 0 \u2264 h \u2264 1 and \u222b h dx = 1.\n- After optimization, refine with the exact evaluator and L-BFGS-B to improve the final solution.\n- Introduce perturbations by adding Gaussian noise to h and rescaling, then evaluating with the exact metric.\n- Track the best solution found over iterations and stop when time is exhausted.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Default configuration: sinusoidal initialization, COBYLA with \u03b1=1000, 200 iterations. Use L-BFGS-B for final refinement.\n- Perturbation: add Gaussian noise with \u03c3=0.05 every 10 iterations.\n- Variations: test different initial patterns (e.g., random, piecewise linear) and \u03b1 values (100-1000). If the exact evaluator is too slow, use the surrogate for early refinement.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- COBYLA is efficient and handles bounds/constraints. The initial steps (200 iterations) take ~300s, leaving 300s for refinement.\n- Stop early if time is exhausted. The surrogate is faster but less accurate; the exact evaluator is used only for final evaluation.\n- If the objective becomes unstable (nan values), clip h to [0,1] and rescale. For solver failures, revert to the best solution so far.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Re-evaluate the final candidate using the exact objective and check \u222b h dx = 1.0.\n- Ensure all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and the C\u2085 bound is finite.\n- Output (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) with n_points=200.",
